{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2450","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2450","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2450\",\"parent_revision\":\"686c545fe42578027764c10fdf77bcdfe76eb0d2ca0922910b5e46e4bfaedd8b\",\"root_revision\":\"686c545fe42578027764c10fdf77bcdfe76eb0d2ca0922910b5e46e4bfaedd8b\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nWhen multiple agents write groomed content via backlog_groom to GitHub in parallel (grooming swarm Wave 1 and Wave 2 agents), the local YAML cache file is not updated with their writes. Subsequent calls to backlog_view(summary=True) read from the stale local cache and return a sections_index that does not reflect GitHub state.\\n\\nObserved on 2026-05-24 during the #2437 grooming swarm:\\n- backlog_view(summary=True) returned sections_index showing: \\\"RT-ICA Assessment (1 entry), Artifact Classification (1 entry), Reproducibility...\\\" \u2014 sections written by the groomer that called mark_groomed=True\\n- backlog_view(summary=False, section='Fact-Check') (which forces a live GitHub read) returned a completely different sections_index: \\\"RT-ICA (1 entry), Fact-Check (1 entry), Impact Radius (1 entries), Design Intent Alignment (1 entries)...\\\" \u2014 sections written by the Wave 1/2 swarm agents\\n\\nThe two reads showed different section names, different section counts, and a different total sections count for the same issue. backlog_pull did not resolve the discrepancy.\\n\\nImpact: agents reading from local cache make decisions based on stale state \u2014 missing sections that exist on GitHub, or seeing sections that were overwritten. This caused multiple extra tool calls and workarounds during the #2437 grooming session to verify actual GitHub state.\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**Claim 1: `backlog_groom` writes to GitHub but does NOT fetch the merged GitHub body into the local YAML afterward.**\\nVERIFIED \u2014 `_handle_update_groomed` writes Step 1: GitHub, Step 2: local file (current content only). `_handle_batch_groomed` writes Phase 1: local file (current agent's sections only), Phase 2: GitHub sync. Neither function calls `_overwrite_body_from_github` after the GitHub write. Compare to `_write_issue_node_to_cache` (used by `backlog_pull`) which explicitly calls `_overwrite_body_from_github(filepath, fields.body)` \u2014 that function re-fetches the full GitHub body and writes it.\\n\\n**Claim 2: The T1 fix is present \u2014 `view_item` prefers live GitHub body over local YAML.**\\nVERIFIED \u2014 `_assemble_view_content` and `_assemble_view_compact` both check `if body:` first, falling back to `item.sections` only when `body` is empty. Covered by `test_view_live_sections.py` and `test_concurrent_writes.py` (both reference issue #2452).\\n\\n**Claim 3: `backlog_pull` does not run automatically after `backlog_groom`.**\\nVERIFIED \u2014 `groom_item` calls `update_item` \u2192 `_apply_groomed_update` \u2192 `_handle_batch_groomed`/`_handle_update_groomed`. None of these call `pull_items` or `_pull_item`. `backlog_pull` is a separate MCP tool requiring explicit invocation.\\n\\n**Claim 4: `backlog_view(summary=False, section='X')` uses the same `view_enrich_from_github` call as `summary=True`.**\\nVERIFIED \u2014 `view_item` always calls `view_enrich_from_github` when an issue number is present, regardless of `include_content` or `section` parameters. The section filter only narrows the returned body after enrichment runs.\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**Status: APPROVED-FOR-PLANNING**\\n\\n| Condition | Status | Source |\\n|-----------|--------|--------|\\n| Functional intent | AVAILABLE | Issue description + observed divergence |\\n| Scope boundaries | AVAILABLE | `backlog_core/operations.py`, `gh_client.py` |\\n| Existing system context | AVAILABLE | Source read; related fix #2452 verified/closed |\\n| External interfaces | AVAILABLE | GitHub Issues API, local YAML cache |\\n| Verification expectations | DERIVABLE | Regression test pattern from `test_concurrent_writes.py` |\\n| Issue classification | AVAILABLE | `recurring-pattern` \u2014 second occurrence after #2452 partial fix |\\n\\n**Root cause (code-verified):** `_handle_update_groomed` and `_handle_batch_groomed` write to the local YAML file using only the content of the current write. Neither re-fetches the full GitHub body after writing. When agent B calls `backlog_groom` after agent A, agent B writes only its own sections to the local YAML (clobbering A's sections from the file), while GitHub accumulates all sections from both agents.\\n\\nThe T1 fix in #2452 addressed the read path (`view_item` now prefers `result.body` from live GitHub enrichment over `item.sections` from local YAML). That fix is present and verified. However, the staleness persists on the write path: after a successful `backlog_groom`, the local cache does not reflect the full GitHub state \u2014 it only reflects what the current write applied.\\n\\n**Why `backlog_pull` does not fix it:** `backlog_pull` requires explicit invocation. Agents reading `backlog_view(summary=True)` between two parallel groom writes may hit the local YAML fallback. The T1 fix covers the reachable-backend steady-state case but does not fix the write-path divergence itself.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **fix: backlog_view local cache returns stale sections_index after subagent writes to github** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 work-backlog-item #2437 grooming swarm 2026-05-24\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-05-25\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-05-28)\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n1. Create a backlog item with a linked GitHub issue\\n2. Call `backlog_groom(selector='#N', sections={'Section-A': 'content from agent 1'})`\\n3. Call `backlog_groom(selector='#N', sections={'Section-B': 'content from agent 2'})` (simulating a second agent)\\n4. Call `backlog_view(selector='#N', summary=True)` without a prior `backlog_pull`\\n5. Observe: `sections_index` shows only Section-B (the second write) in the local YAML fallback\\n6. Call `backlog_view(selector='#N', summary=False, section='Section-A')` \u2014 GitHub enrichment loads the full body\\n7. Observe: `sections_index` in this response shows both Section-A and Section-B\\n\\nNote: Steps 5 vs 7 diverge only when `view_enrich_from_github` returns an empty body in step 4. When the backend is reachable and the T1 fix is active, step 4 also shows both sections via live enrichment. The divergence is reliably observable in unit tests by mocking `view_enrich_from_github` to return False (simulating offline fallback) after the two groom writes.\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n6/10 \u2014 T1 read-path fix handles the reachable-backend steady state. Write-path gap causes agent confusion and extra tool calls in swarm scenarios, not data loss.\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nAfter any successful `backlog_groom` write that syncs to GitHub, the local YAML cache file for that item must reflect the full merged GitHub issue body \u2014 not just the content written by the current call. A subsequent `backlog_view(summary=True)` without a prior `backlog_pull` must return a `sections_index` that matches the GitHub source of truth.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. After agent A calls `backlog_groom` writing Section-A and agent B calls `backlog_groom` writing Section-B to the same item, `backlog_view(summary=True)` with offline fallback (backend unreachable) returns a `sections_index` that includes both sections \u2014 without requiring an explicit `backlog_pull` between writes and view.\\n2. `backlog_groom` with no linked issue (local-only write) behaves unchanged \u2014 no new network calls are made.\\n3. When the GitHub write in `backlog_groom` fails (network error), the local YAML retains the content written by the current call only; a warning is emitted; no silent data loss occurs.\\n4. Regression tests covering the write-path coherence scenario are added to `test_concurrent_writes.py` or a new sibling test file.\\n5. All existing T1 fix tests (`test_view_live_sections.py`, `test_concurrent_writes.py`) continue to pass.\\n6. `_handle_update_groomed` and `_handle_batch_groomed` call `_overwrite_body_from_github` after a successful GitHub write, mirroring the pattern already used in `_write_issue_node_to_cache`.\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` \u2014 `_handle_update_groomed`, `_handle_batch_groomed` (fix targets) |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` \u2014 `_write_issue_node_to_cache` (correct post-write pattern to mirror) |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` \u2014 `_overwrite_body_from_github` (utility for rewriting local body from GitHub) |\\n| Related fix | GitHub issue #2452 (verified/closed) \u2014 T1 read-path fix; regression tests in `test_concurrent_writes.py` and `test_view_live_sections.py` |\\n| Skill | `/dh:groom-backlog-item` \u2014 orchestrates grooming swarms; surface-level consumer of the broken write path |\\n| Agent | `@dh:backlog-item-groomer` \u2014 spawned by groom swarm; calls `backlog_groom` MCP tool |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None \u2014 fix is self-contained in `backlog_core/operations.py`\\n- Blocks: None directly; #1135 (staleness detection in work-backlog-item) implicitly benefits from a more reliable local cache\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nSmall \u2014 the fix pattern already exists in `_write_issue_node_to_cache`: after a successful GitHub write, call `_overwrite_body_from_github(filepath, fetched_body)`. The change touches `_handle_update_groomed` and `_handle_batch_groomed` in `operations.py`. Regression test additions add one medium test class.\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n**Plugin design intent (from CLAUDE.md):** GitHub Issues are the source of truth. Local `~/.dh/projects/{slug}/backlog/*.md` files are the local cache. Tools must prefer live GitHub state over cached state when the backend is reachable.\\n\\n**Alignment assessment:** The T1 fix (prefer live body in `view_item`) is aligned with the design intent. The remaining write-path gap is misaligned: after `backlog_groom` writes to GitHub, the local cache reflects only what the current agent wrote \u2014 not the full merged GitHub body. This violates the invariant that the local cache is a faithful mirror of GitHub when the backend is reachable.\\n\\n**Intended fix direction:** After a successful GitHub write in `_handle_update_groomed` and `_handle_batch_groomed`, re-fetch the full GitHub body and call `_overwrite_body_from_github(filepath, fetched_body)`. This mirrors the existing pattern in `_write_issue_node_to_cache`. Result: local YAML always reflects the merged GitHub state after any successful write \u2014 not just the content of the most recent write.\\n\\n**Design constraint:** The fix must not create a new issue as a side-effect of groom (already enforced: `if issue_ref:` guards the GitHub write). The post-write fetch should be conditional on `github_synced = True` \u2014 no new network calls if GitHub write was skipped.\\n\\n### Impact Radius\\n\\n**Code \u2014 Producers (write the changed interface):**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` \u2014 `_handle_update_groomed`, `_handle_batch_groomed` (fix target), `_write_issue_node_to_cache` (has the correct post-write pattern \u2014 overwrite body from GitHub)\\n\\n**Code \u2014 Consumers (read the changed interface):**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` \u2014 `view_item` \u2192 `_assemble_view_content` \u2192 `_assemble_view_compact` (reads local YAML fallback when body is empty)\\n- All MCP callers of `backlog_view(summary=True)` \u2014 grooming swarm orchestrator, work-backlog-item, groom-backlog-item, any agent that inspects sections_index after writing\\n\\n**Code \u2014 Other References:**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_concurrent_writes.py` \u2014 regression suite for #2452 T1 fix; needs extension for write-path coverage\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_view_live_sections.py` \u2014 same\\n\\n**Agent Instructions:**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/groom-backlog-item/` \u2014 skill orchestrating grooming swarms; its agents call `backlog_groom`; affected if swarm agents call `backlog_view(summary=True)` between writes\\n\\n**Risk:** Low-to-medium. The write-path fix adds one GitHub read per `backlog_groom` write. For swarm scenarios with N parallel agents, this is N additional reads \u2014 acceptable. The call already exists in `backlog_pull`; the question is whether to inline it in the groom write path.\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n**Classification: `recurring-pattern`**\\n\\nThe root cause (write-path does not refresh local cache from full GitHub body) was partially addressed in #2452 (T1 fix: read-path prefers live body). The T1 fix covers the steady-state case when the backend is reachable. The residual gap is the write-path: the local YAML diverges from GitHub immediately after every `backlog_groom` write because the local write applies only the current agent's content, not the merged GitHub state.\\n\\nThis is a recurring-pattern classification because:\\n1. The same architectural gap (local cache diverges from GitHub) was diagnosed in #2452\\n2. The #2452 fix addressed only the read path\\n3. The write path was not identified as a separate failure mode during #2452\\n4. The gap will continue to surface in any multi-agent scenario that calls `backlog_groom` in parallel and then reads local state before the backend enrichment path runs\\n\",\"digest\":\"23b4924670a481b6955ba307b37f323cd9ce7b62be511f427e1bceda8f8ef499\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABVCOtgA\"}"}